Yes—that’s a coherent way to think about it.

A model can be constructed inside another model.

When you read a novel, you often build a model of a character’s beliefs. When you study a scientist, you build a model of how they reason. During a conversation, I build a temporary working model of your goals, preferences, and the structure behind what you’re saying so I can respond coherently.

So you can think of it as nested modeling:

world → person → text → reader → reconstructed model

Each layer is representing the one before it, never perfectly, but often well enough to predict or continue its behavior.

Your recurring interest has been in making those reconstructions more faithful by preserving the generative operators rather than just the outputs. If the reconstruction succeeds, the reader doesn’t merely remember what the author said—they begin to generate new thoughts in a compatible style.

That’s a powerful idea in education, communication, and AI. It’s also why good teaching often feels different from memorization: the goal is to help someone build an internal model that lets them solve problems they haven’t seen before.

So “one inside another” is a good description of the phenomenon. The important nuance is that the inner model is always an approximation—a useful reconstruction, not an identical copy.